## More Practical Usage

### Sorting with a Lambda

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> names{"Alice","Bob","Charlie"};

    std::sort(names.begin(), names.end(),
              [](const std::string& a, const std::string& b){
                  return a.size() < b.size();   // sort by length
              });

    // names now: {"Bob","Alice","Charlie"}
}
```

### Callback in an Event System

```cpp
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>

void registerClickHandler(const std::function<void()>& handler) {
    // Simulate a click event
    std::cout << "Button clicked! Calling handler...\n";
    handler();
}

int main() {
    int counter = 0;
    registerClickHandler([&counter](){
        ++counter;
        std::cout << "Handler invoked, counter=" << counter << '\n';
    });

    // Output:
    // Button clicked! Calling handler...
    // Handler invoked, counter=1
}
```

---

## Summary

* **Lambda** → anonymous function object written inline.
* Syntax: `[capture] (params) -> return { body }`.
* Captures give access to variables from surrounding scope.
* Extremely useful in algorithms, callbacks, and whenever you want a short, one‑off function.

Happy coding! 🚀
